{"model": "/Users/zeng/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--mlx-community--granite-3.3-8b-instruct-8bit/snapshots/265b90718032d76d98f71715f389fdd597db55b4", "mode": "chatx", "char": "CAT", "cap": "memory", "dim": "agency", "ev": 4.000000367723136, "digit_mass": 0.9999994039535522, "probs": [1.4000923753698657e-09, 1.3077761984447989e-08, 1.0008996298438433e-07, 4.2893134377663955e-06, 0.9999910593032837, 4.2893134377663955e-06, 2.037730695292339e-07, 5.295013849604402e-08, 6.67846999924393e-10, 4.82914819244229e-10], "top_token": "4", "reply": "Cats possess remarkable memory skills for animals, though not on a human level. They can remember specific people, places, and events, demonstrating an ability to recall information over extended periods. For instance, cats can remember positive and negative experiences, adjusting their behavior accordingly. Studies have shown that cats can remember a sequence of events and even learn to navigate mazes effectively. However, their memory capabilities are instinctual and tied to survival, such as recalling the location of food sources or safe hiding spots, rather than abstract or complex human-like recollections. \n\nRating: 4. While cats show impressive memory for survival and learned behaviors, their memory systems are fundamentally different from humans, lacking the capacity for abstract thought or long-term semantic memory. \n\nRating: 4.", "parse": "marker"}
